<%# Temporary hardcoded content for the PostalVoteVideo A/B test. %>
<div class="app-c-postal-vote-video">
  <p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Abl8WliyXSc">What is a postal vote?</a></p>

  <h2 id="what-is-a-postal-vote">What is a postal vote?</h2>

  <p>
    This video explains what postal voting is, who can apply for a postal vote
    and what happens to your vote on polling day.
  </p>

  <p>GOV.UK • 1 minute 48 seconds</p>

  <%= render "govuk_publishing_components/components/details", {
    title: "Transcript",
  } do %>
    <p>
      A postal vote allows you to vote without having to visit a polling
      station. Instead of voting in person, your ballot paper will be sent to
      you by post a few weeks before an election. You simply mark your choice
      on the ballot paper, complete and sign the postal voting statement, seal
      everything in the pre-paid envelope, and return it by post.
    </p>

    <p>
      Anyone can apply, and if you’re voting in England, Scotland, or Wales,
      you don’t need to give a reason why you want to vote by post.
    </p>

    <p>
      To apply for a postal vote, you must be registered to vote first. If
      you’re not already registered, you can do this on the GOV.UK website.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you need a postal vote for an upcoming election, you’ll need to apply
      by 5pm, 11 days before polling day. Applying on GOV.UK takes a few
      minutes and you’ll need to know your National Insurance number, which
      you can find on a payslip, on letters from HMRC, or on GOV.UK by
      searching for ‘find my national insurance number.’ As a part of the
      application, you’ll also need to upload an image of your signature which
      will be used to verify your identity each time you vote - so make sure
      you have a pen and paper to hand.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once you’ve applied, your ballot paper will arrive by post before polling
      day. You should complete it and post it back as quickly as possible, so
      that your vote gets counted. If you miss the deadline to return it, you
      can find out what to do on GOV.UK.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once you post it back, your ballot paper will be securely stored and then
      counted alongside all the other votes, once polls close on election day.
    </p>

    <p>To apply, search ‘apply postal vote’ on GOV.UK.</p>
  <% end %>
</div>
